# @@NAME@@

A [collage](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage) project, scaffolded by
`collage new`.

## Running

```
go mod tidy
go run .
```

Or, using the collage CLI from inside this directory: `collage dev`.

## The collage CLI contract

This project's `main.go` implements both halves of the contract the `collage`
CLI relies on:

- **`collage dev`** runs `go run .` in this directory with `COLLAGE_DEV=1` set
  in the process environment. `main.go` reads that variable and turns on
  development mode, which reloads templates from disk on every request. It
  does **not** hot-reload Go code — a change to `main.go`, or any other `.go`
  file here, still requires stopping and re-running `collage dev` (or
  `go run .`).
- **`collage build`** runs `go run . -collage-build -out <dir>` (appending
  `-clean` when `collage build -clean` was used). `main.go` parses those flags
  and, when `-collage-build` is set, renders every statically-buildable page
  to `<dir>` instead of starting a server, through `collage.NewBuilder` —
  collage-core's own static builder, so this project gets the same
  output-directory safety checks (symlink-escape containment, refusing a
  dangerous `-out`) as every other project built from this scaffold, rather
  than a copy of its own.

If you rewrite `main.go`, keep both halves of that contract working, or
`collage dev` / `collage build` will stop doing anything useful for this
project.

## Layout

- `go.mod` — this project's own module, depending on `pkg/collage`.
- `main.go` — wires the app and its one page, and implements the CLI contract
  above.
- `templates/layouts/default.html` — the page shell; `{{slot "content"}}`
  marks where a page's content fragment is rendered.
- `templates/pages/home.html` — the home page's content, served at `/`.

